The equilibrium polarization for a spin‑½ nucleus is  

\[
P \approx \frac{\hbar \gamma B}{2kT}
\]

For \(^{13}\mathrm{C}\)  
\(\gamma = 2\pi \times 10.705\times10^{6}\,\text{rad s}^{-1}\text{T}^{-1} = 6.72\times10^{7}\,\text{rad s}^{-1}\text{T}^{-1}\)

\[
P = \frac{(1.055\times10^{-34}\,\text{J·s})(6.72\times10^{7}\,\text{rad s}^{-1}\text{T}^{-1})(20.0\,\text{T})}
{2(1.381\times10^{-23}\,\text{J K}^{-1})(300\,\text{K})}
\approx 1.7\times10^{-5}
\]

Thus the equilibrium polarization is \(1.71 \times 10^{-5}\).

Answer: D
